codemap 9
tour feature-scroll-code
kind flow
author ai
group features/gui
note The reader meets code wider than where it is shown - a long line in the Source view, a tour step in the Tour document, a reference's code, all more common in a split panel - and needs to see the end of the line. Without this the line is cut at the panel edge with nothing showing more exists (a run on a file of 250-column lines shows it), and the only way sideways was a hidden shift+wheel that read only the vertical wheel: a trackpad's sideways swipe and macOS's shift+mouse-wheel, which arrives as a horizontal delta, did nothing, and the same wheel also scrolled the column around the block. It serves the first goal, reading unfamiliar code through codemap with less friction than an editor. Nothing else answers it: code is never wrapped, so a line stays one row and line numbers, anchors and selections hold, and the graph's pan moves only the graph. Every code block wider than its rectangle now has a horizontal scrollbar along the bottom of the part of it that shows, with room made under its last line: drag the thumb, click the track to jump, swipe sideways, or shift+wheel, which keys::turn_wheel turns sideways once per frame from the registry's ShiftWheel gesture so the column does not also scroll. The offset is kept per block in the Model's across scrolls. The bar is the ui's one scrollbar, opted into per element with Scrollbars::HORIZONTAL, so both directions look and behave the same. Of three prototypes this one was kept: a bar drawn and hit-tested by the code widget (block) and one bar per panel moving all its blocks together (panel) each needed a second implementation of thumb drag and track click beside the ui's, and the panel's bar sat far below a short document and shifted steps at different indents by the same pixels.

